  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Day to day warming continues, with fair weather \ntoday, small chance for showers Saturday.\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure is centered across our region \nthis morning, with light winds and Southern Tier valleys filling \nwith fog.  \n\nThis feature will maintain fair weather with sunny skies today, with \na partly to mostly sunny sky Saturday as a little mid level moisture \nsneaks northward from OH/PA. A spot shower or thunderstorm will be \npossible along a Lake Erie lake breeze circulation Saturday \nafternoon, but overall much of our region will remain dry. \n\nHighs in the lower 80s today will become mid 80s for Saturday. \nDewpoints, however, will remain relatively cool through Saturday, \nwith values only nearing 60F, resulting in a not so humid airmass. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather returns Sunday and into the \nbeginning of the new work week. \n\nSeveral convective shortwave troughs cresting a Rockies/Plains ridge \nof high pressure combined with the deepening upper level low near \nHudson Bay will return unsettled weather to our region by Sunday. \n\nSoutherly flow behind the recently departed surface high pressure \nsystem will advect deeper moisture (increasing humidity) across the \neastern Great Lakes with Pwat values reaching 1.5 inches or greater. \n\nBuilding instability across WNY will encourage convection through \nthe day with scattered showers and thunderstorms developing, \nespecially along lake breeze boundaries. Additional shortwave \ntroughs within a northwest flow will maintain showers and \nthunderstorms, especially during the peak heating of the day, across \nour region Monday and into middle portion of next week. \n\nIt should be noted that there will be a good deal of time rain-free \nearly next week, and the uneven coverage of convection will allow \nfor some areas to be poured upon while other areas remain dry. \n\nFor severe potential, northwest flow can be a direction that can be \nan active pattern for localized severe thunderstorm development \nacross the Lower Great Lakes. For now, the core of the LLJ and \ngreater 0-6km bulk shear magnitudes appears to be to our west and \nsouth, which may limit the severe potential for our region.",
